## 5. Lands in

- TARGET `src/fight/actor-clips.ts` (new module). Creature clip clock: which clip plays, how far through it, and when the next frame is due. Parity-checklist row 7 (creature actor/clips).
- Best transferable idea: drive clip frames from elapsed time against a per-clip frame duration with the repeat count as data, and make a step's visual progress elapsed/duration so a late update neither skips nor stretches a move.
- Decision (proposed): adapt. Take the elapsed-time frame clock and the progress fraction; reject the sprite direction mirror and the pixel offsets (Frankendom's actors are 3D); do not copy the one-frame-per-call behaviour, which slows clips when the frame rate drops.
- Acceptance: a unit test drives a 3-frame, 80 ms clip with 16 ms updates and then one 250 ms gap; the frame shown at 250 ms is `floor(250 / 80) mod 3`, not one step later. A player at 20 fps sees the same swing frame at the same time as at 60 fps.
- Reuse tag: rewrite-from-idea (BSD-2-Clause, code may be adapted; written as a clean sketch anyway)
- Brief rule served: 1 (one fight engine, actors are clients of its state); row 7; the clip never decides hit timing (server-verified kills stay in the engine).

## 6. Gaps and risks

- The donor's clock is per-call, not per-time. Frankendom must not copy it: a dropped frame would stretch every swing, and that changes the feel of a timed attack.
- The clip choice is a table of numeric group ids keyed by graphic type. Frankendom's ruling is zones and catalogue as data (catalogue fields, row 21), so clip names and durations go in the creature record, not in a numeric table.
- Server-driven timing (interval scales the frame time) is a wire-format detail of UO. Frankendom needs one explicit per-attack field (`frameMs` or `interval`), set in data, not inferred from a packet.
- The donor's visual step offset is client prediction and snaps to the server tile on removal. In Frankendom the engine owns position; the actor may lerp for display, but a hit window must come from the engine tick, not from the clip frame or the offset.
- Repeat semantics: the donor's restart-then-loop behaviour (traced, not run) looks unintended. Do not copy it; use a plain "play N times, then hold last frame or go idle" rule.
- The ClassicUO renderer is 2D sprites with no skeleton. Only the clock and the state-to-clip mapping transfer; any bone or skin logic is absent.
- `FrameDelay[1]` (the value subtracted from step time) was not read, so the donor's exact step timing differs from the sketch by that amount.
